text:
A whole page
FastHTML’s test Client fetches a page without starting a server. In the response, &lt;head&gt; contains htmx, the AnswerUI stylesheet, the event script, and the inline browser.js module. The spritesheet appears at the end of &lt;body&gt;, after the icon that references it.

app = FastHTML(hdrs=reg.hdrs(), ftrs=reg.ftrs())
rt = app.route

@rt
def index(): return Titled('Hello', LateCard(Check(), 'Defined after the app.'))

page = Client(app).get('/').text
assert page.index('&lt;symbol id="lc-check"') &gt; page.index('late-card')
print(re.sub(r'(?s)(&lt;(style|script)\b[^&gt;]*&gt;).*?(?=&lt;/\2&gt;)', r'\1…', page))

&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Hello&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;link rel="canonical" href="https://testserver/"&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
    &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover"&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/htmx.org@2.0.7/dist/htmx.js"&gt;…&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/answerdotai/fasthtml-js@1.0.13/fasthtml.js"&gt;…&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/answerdotai/surreal@main/surreal.js"&gt;…&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/gnat/css-scope-inline@main/script.js"&gt;…&lt;/script&gt;    &lt;style&gt;…&lt;/style&gt;
&lt;script&gt;…&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script type="module"&gt;…&lt;/script&gt;  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
&lt;main class="container"&gt;      &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;late-card&gt;&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewbox="0 0 24 24" height="24px" width="24px" class="lucide-icon "&gt;&lt;use href="#lc-check"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;Defined after the app.&lt;/late-card&gt;&lt;/main&gt;    &lt;style&gt;…&lt;/style&gt;
&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="display: none"&gt;&lt;defs&gt;&lt;symbol id="lc-check"&gt;&lt;path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/symbol&gt;&lt;/defs&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;



With fast_app, pass pico=False to disable Pico CSS:

app, rt = fast_app(hdrs=reg.hdrs(), ftrs=reg.ftrs(), pico=False)
@rt
def index(): return Titled('Hello', LateCard('Defined after the app.'))

assert 'pico' not in (r:= Client(app).get('/').text)
assert 'late-card' in r

Demo
Below is a minimal stylesheet using the generated ladder tokens to style a card:

from answerui.preview import preview
from fasthtml.common import Article, H3, P, Em, A, Hr, Button, to_xml

def demo(*items, css): return preview(to_xml(*items), css=css, height='13rem')


demo_css = 'body, article{' + ladder_css() + '''}
body{--hue:260;--neutral-hue:260;color-scheme:light;background:var(--n-3);color:var(--n2);margin:0;padding:1.5rem;font-family:system-ui}
article{background:var(--n-2);border:1px solid var(--n-1);border-radius:6px;padding:1rem;max-width:18rem}
a{color:var(--v)}
hr{border:none;border-top:1px solid var(--n-1)}
button{background:var(--v);color:var(--n-3);border:none;border-radius:6px;padding:.5rem 1rem;
  &:hover{background:var(--v1)}
  &[secondary]{background:var(--v-3);color:var(--v2);border:1px solid var(--v-1)}
}
'''

def payment_card(**kw):
    return Article(
        H3('Payment method'),
        P('Your card ending in ', Em('4242'), ' expires soon. ', A('See all cards', href='#'), '.'),
        Hr(),
        Button('Update card'), ' ', Button('Remind me later', secondary=True),
        **kw)

demo(payment_card(), css=demo_css)

Replay preview


Overriding --hue for a danger state changes links and buttons while leaving the card surface and body text in the cool neutral ramp. Override --neutral-hue as well to tint the neutral ramp to match. Because the browser evaluates CSS custom property formulas on the declaring element before inheritance, palette.css redeclares the ladder tokens on :root, [ui], [ui-tone], and [prose] mark.

demo_css += 'article[danger]{--hue:25}'
demo(payment_card(danger=True), css=demo_css)

Replay preview


Because each token is a light-dark() pair, changing color-scheme switches the page between light and dark colours.

demo(payment_card(), css=demo_css + 'body{color-scheme:dark}')

Replay preview

Shipping
palette_css() writes the generated CSS fragment to nbs/palette.css, which #| asset_include styles.css palette.css folds into the shared stylesheet used by HTML previews, Svelte, and Python. Rerun this notebook after changing any palette target to update the fitted coefficients in nbs/palette.css.
Inside @layer tokens and @scope (:root, [ui]) to ([unstyle]), palette.css defines:

Root defaults: color-scheme: light dark, --hue: 260, --neutral-hue: 260, --hue-danger: 25, --hue-warning: 75, --hue-success: 145, and --hue-info: 230.
Theme overrides: [ui-theme=system], [ui-theme=light], and [ui-theme=dark].
Tone overrides: [ui-tone=danger], [ui-tone=warning] (also applied to [prose] mark), [ui-tone=success], [ui-tone=info], and [ui-tone=neutral] (which remaps --v-3 through --v2 to neutral rungs --n-2 through --n2).
Semantic tokens on :root, [ui], [ui-tone], and [prose] mark: --color-text (--n2), --color-muted (--n1), --color-background (--n-3), --color-surface (--n-2), --color-border (--n-1), --color-accent (--v), --color-on-accent (--n-3), and --ring (2px solid var(--v-1)).

Because CSS evaluates custom-property formulas on the declaring element before inheritance, changing --hue on an ordinary descendant does not recompute --v-3 through --v2 or --color-accent. For standard tones, set ui-tone. When an element overrides --hue dynamically from a custom CSS state rule (for example, switching a button’s hue while [aria-busy="true"] or .htmx-request is active), add ui (ui=True in FastHTML) on that element so the ladder recomputes whenever --hue changes; component rules in @layer components override [ui]’s base rules in @layer base.

def palette_css():
    return """
@layer tokens {
  @scope (:root, [ui]) to ([unstyle]) {
    :where(:scope):root {
      color-scheme: light dark;
      --hue: 260; --neutral-hue: 260;
      --hue-danger: 25; --hue-warning: 75; --hue-success: 145; --hue-info: 230;
    }
    :where(:scope, *) {
      &[ui-theme=system] { color-scheme: light dark; }
      &[ui-theme=light] { color-scheme: light; }
      &[ui-theme=dark] { color-scheme: dark; }
      &[ui-tone=danger] { --hue: var(--hue-danger); }
      &[ui-tone=warning] { --hue: var(--hue-warning); }
      &[ui-tone=success] { --hue: var(--hue-success); }
      &[ui-tone=info] { --hue: var(--hue-info); }
    }
    [prose] mark { --hue: var(--hue-warning); }

    :where(:scope):is(:root, [ui]), :where(:scope, *)[ui-tone], [prose] mark {
""" + indent(ladder_css().rstrip(), '      ') + """
      --color-text: var(--n2); --color-muted: var(--n1);
      --color-background: var(--n-3); --color-surface: var(--n-2); --color-border: var(--n-1);
      --color-accent: var(--v); --color-on-accent: var(--n-3);
      --ring: 2px solid var(--v-1);
    }
    :where(:scope, *)[ui-tone=neutral] {
      --v-3: var(--n-2); --v-2: var(--n-1); --v-1: var(--n); --v: var(--n1); --v1: var(--n2); --v2: var(--n2);
    }
  }
}
""".rstrip() + '\n'

asset = get_config().nbs_path/'palette.css'
css = palette_css().lstrip()
if not asset.exists() or asset.read_text() != css: asset.write_text(css)

Responsive navbars, toolbars, and breadcrumbs
Translated labels and dynamic items can overflow a navbar at widths where a fixed breakpoint does not trigger. watchInlineFit(host, row, onCompact) toggles data-fitting to measure the row’s natural width against the container. Wide and compact states share one labelled &lt;nav&gt; element and its links (a[aria-current="page"] marks the current page). Put [navbar-toggle] on a disclosure button with aria-controls and aria-expanded rather than using an ARIA menu. Without JavaScript, the links remain visible and wrap.
Use [navbar] with [navbar-row], [navbar-brand], &lt;nav&gt;, [navbar-toggle], and [navbar-end]. Optional modifiers are [navbar-align="center"], [navbar-align="end"], [navbar-vivid], and [navbar-sticky]. mountNavbar(host, {open, onOpenChange}) sets data-compact when the row overflows and toggles data-open on the &lt;nav&gt; row. [navbar-brand], [navbar-toggle], and [navbar-end] stay on the first row while &lt;nav&gt; expands below them. mountNavbar returns {setOpen, dispose} and closes the compact &lt;nav&gt; on Escape, link clicks, or outside clicks. Call mountNavbar on a [navbar-sticky] navbar even without [navbar-toggle]. It writes the navbar’s height to --navbar-size on :root for scroll-padding-block-start and removes --navbar-size on dispose().
[responsive-toolbar] and [breadcrumb] use the same data-compact and data-fitting attributes with their own compact markup and focus handling. A [responsive-toolbar] contains [toolbar-actions], [toolbar-group], [toolbar-divider], [toolbar-shortcut], and [toolbar-menu], switching from [toolbar-actions] to [toolbar-menu] when compact. A [breadcrumb] contains [breadcrumb-row], [breadcrumb-parents], [breadcrumb-collapse], [breadcrumb-ancestor], and an ellipsized [breadcrumb-current], with spacing controlled by --breadcrumb-gap (default --space-3). When compact, [breadcrumb-collapse] replaces [breadcrumb-parents].

