Surfaces, status, and tables
Add [card] for a raised --color-surface container with --space padding and a border. Combine [card] with [stack] or another layout attribute when you need spacing between its children. Add [badge] for an inline status label tinted by the active [ui-tone], or badge-variant="secondary" for a --color-surface badge with an inset border. Add [avatar] for a circular image or initials box sized by --avatar-size (default 2.5rem). When [avatar] is the only child of [btn], the button becomes a circular 1px-padded --color-surface frame.
Use [alert] with [ui-tone] for persistent notices instead of transient button states. Inside [alert], a direct child [stack] fills the remaining row width with --space-2 gaps. Add [progress] to style a native &lt;progress&gt; bar at full width with --color-accent. Add [spinner] to show a 1em spinning indicator before its label. Under prefers-reduced-motion: reduce, the animation stops. Add [skeleton] for a full-width placeholder bar (1em high) in --n-1.
Add [table] to a &lt;table&gt; (table[table]) for row borders, start-aligned cells, bold headers and footer headings, and a bottom caption. Add [num] to numeric &lt;th&gt; and &lt;td&gt; cells for end alignment and tabular digits (tabular-nums). Wrap table[table] in [table-frame] for a bordered, horizontally scrolling container with --color-surface headers and white-space: nowrap cells. Give [table-frame] role="region", tabindex="0", and aria-label so keyboard users can scroll it. Plain &lt;table&gt; elements inside [prose] use the prose table styles instead.

